# Grandway scheduled jobs — CRON FALLBACK ONLY.
#
# The preferred mechanism is the systemd timer pair shipped alongside this
# file (deploy/grandway-sweep.service + deploy/grandway-sweep.timer), which
# runs on the Nepal clock, logs to journald, and catches up missed runs. Use
# this crontab only on a host without systemd timers. Install with:
#
#     crontab -u root -e        (root: manage.sh re-executes as grandway itself)
#
# See deploy.md §13 (Scheduled jobs).
#
# Notification sweep — nightly at 02:15 NPT. Cron uses the HOST clock; on a
# UTC host (the default) 02:15 Asia/Kathmandu (UTC+05:45) is 20:30 UTC, which
# is what the line below says. Adjust if the host runs on another timezone.
#
# manage.sh loads /etc/grandway/grandway.env, exports ENVIRONMENT=production,
# refuses to run if a stray /opt/grandway/.env exists, and always runs as the
# grandway user — so no ENVIRONMENT= or PATH= lines are needed here and no
# root-owned app.log is ever created.
#
# Raises alerts (checklist due/overdue, missing documents, offer expiry and
# response deadlines, passport expiry, custom reminders) and resolves alerts
# whose condition has cleared. Idempotent: every write is a get_or_create on a
# (recipient, dedupe_key) unique constraint, so re-running is safe.
#
# KNOWN LIMITATION (1.0.0): the command exits 0 even when individual generators
# fail — it reports to stderr but does not signal failure, so cron's own MAILTO
# will not flag a partially failed night. Output is piped to syslog under the
# tag grandway-sweep; monitor `journalctl -t grandway-sweep -p warning`.
30 20 * * * /opt/grandway/deploy/manage.sh sweep_notifications 2>&1 | logger -t grandway-sweep
